# todo/t1388 — a BARE LOCAL BIND of a refcount handle passes the SLOT ADDRESS to
# a by-VALUE incref. No call, no container, no sigil: two names and a read.
#
# `Shared__T__clone` takes its handle BY VALUE while `Shared__T__drop` takes it
# BY ADDRESS, and the bind emits `clone(&__s1)`. `strong` is field 0, so the
# incref adds 1 to the bytes of the pointer variable `__s1` ITSELF, and the
# "clone" it returns is the address of `a`'s own stack slot. Hence BOTH rows
# below, from one defect:
#
#   * reading `a` after the bind  -> the corrupted handle -> SIGSEGV
#   * reading `b` after the bind  -> `a`'s stack slot as a handle -> a garbage
#     integer at rc 0, on BOTH backends. SILENT WRONG OUTPUT, no crash. An
#     executor who fixes the crash and stops leaves this one standing, which is
#     why both reads are in this fixture rather than only the loud one.
#
# This is the SAME class as the closed `t0840` (DONE.md 2026-08-31), whose fix
# added THREE consumers of `is_refcount_clone_type_name` — the container write,
# the receiver, and the element read — and left the bare-bind path with none:
#   grep -c "is_refcount_clone_type" src/ir/lowering/stmts/mod.rs   -> 0
# `a.clone()` prints the intended `7 / 7` precisely BECAUSE the explicit-clone
# path does consult the predicate (`src/ir/lowering/exprs/methods.rs`).
#
# INTENDED: the incref takes the handle by VALUE, `b` is an independent handle
# on the same control block, and both names read 7.
from std.sync import Shared

void main():
    Shared[int] a = Shared[int](7)
    Shared[int] b = a
    print(f"{a.get()}")
    print(f"{b.get()}")
